course: dictionary
lesson: 10338
title: Trample: stepping on and crushing
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: To trample something is to step heavily on it so that it is crushed or damaged. You can find the meaning from the words around it: «Cows with calves can be very protective and can trample people.» Protective and people getting hurt are clues that trample means something heavy and dangerous.
explain: Use the swap check. Read «Big grazers like bison eat, trample and rub against young trees» and put in the meaning: bison eat, step heavily on and crush, and rub against young trees. It still makes sense, so you have the right meaning. The trees are damaged because the bison crush them.
explain: Trample has a second meaning. To trample on someone's feelings or rights is to treat them as if they do not matter. Nobody is literally stepping on anything here. The word shows that something important is being ignored or hurt.
explain: Look at the words that go with trample. Trample on and trampled underfoot are clues. Keep off the grass so you don't trample plants: this is the physical meaning, and the sentence tells you the grass and plants could be damaged.
fact: To trample something means to step heavily on it and crush or damage it.
fact: «Cows with calves can be very protective and can trample people.» Stay well away from cows with calves.
fact: In a crowd, people can be trampled, so stay calm and keep your own space.
fact: «Big grazers like bison eat, trample and rub against young trees, opening sunny clearings in a forest where other plants and animals can live.»
fact: To trample on someone's feelings or rights means to treat them as if they do not matter.
exercise: «Cows with calves can be very protective and can trample people.» What does trample mean here? | *Step heavily on and crush or hurt | Look after carefully | Walk slowly beside | Run away from
exercise: «Big grazers like bison eat, trample and rub against young trees.» What do the bison do to the young trees? | *Step heavily on them and damage them | Give them shade | Water them every day | Count them carefully
exercise: Ravi says, «Mocking my drawing tramples on my feelings.» What does trample on feelings mean? | Walk on the grass near a drawing | Paint over a drawing | *Treat someone's feelings as if they do not matter | Sing a loud song
test: Keep off the grass so you don't trample plants. What does trample mean in this line? | Pick | *Step heavily on and crush | Cut | Water
test: Jess is in a crowd, and she knows a crush can trample people. What is the safest thing for her to do? | Run into the middle of the crowd | Push quickly to the front | Shout at everyone | *Stay calm and keep her space
exam: Which sentence uses trample in the feelings sense? | The bison trampled the young trees | The cows trampled the grass flat | *Mocking his efforts would trample on his feelings | The crowd trampled the flowers
